chx:F1 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Background"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Chapter 15 of the 3rd edition, Google Sheets, teaches the third-party EZSheets library in eight sections: installing and setting up (a Google Cloud project, the Sheets and Drive APIs, the OAuth consent screen, a credentials file and token files, and revoking them), spreadsheet objects (creating, opening by id or URL, uploading, listing, the attributes title, id, url, sheetTitles and sheets, downloading in six formats, deleting), sheet objects (reading and writing cells by address, whole rows and columns, the sizes rowCount and columnCount, creating, moving, deleting, clearing and copying sheets), Google Forms with Project 11, a fake blockchain kept in a sheet with an audit program and a transaction program, working with quotas, a summary, 10 practice questions and 3 practice programs." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01 .
chx:F2 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's results that can be checked without Google's servers hold: the audit program of Project 11, run on the public Boringcoin sheet as exported on 2026-10-01 (1001 rows of three columns), prints the same 20 balances in the same order as the chapter and the total 1000000000; practice program 3, run on the export of the Bean Count sheet (15000 rows under a header), finds one wrong total, in row 14399, where 804 times 623 is 500892 and the sheet shows 500891; and getColumnLetterOf(999) is ALK and getColumnNumberOf('ZZZ') is 18278, both for the library's functions and for a version written for this study, which agree for the column numbers 1 to 200000." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P02, chx:P09, chx:P10 .
chx:F3 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Three printed transcripts of the chapter differ in EZSheets 2026.4.28, the newest release on 2026-10-01, run against a stand-in for Google's servers: ss.Sheet('New blank sheet') prints nothing because Spreadsheet.Sheet returns None, while createSheet returns the new Sheet object; a number just written, sheet['B2'] = 30, reads back as the number 30 and becomes the text '30' only after a refresh, where the chapter prints '30'; and the default file name of a download is made from the title with spaces replaced, so the title Sweigart Books (DO NOT DELETE) of the chapter's own transcript gives Sweigart_Books_(DO_NOT_DELETE).xlsx and not the printed Sweigart_Books.xlsx." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P02 .
chx:F4 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Two behaviours of the library are not in the chapter: updateRow and updateRows pad the list they are given with empty strings in place, and updateRows also extends the list of rows to the sheet's size, so the caller's list has grown after the call (a list of 4 became a list of 26, and a list of 2 rows one of 1001), which the source of updateColumn and updateColumns does in the same way but was not executed; and getIdFromUrl, which opens a spreadsheet given by URL, cuts the id by position and drops the id's last character when nothing follows the id in the URL, so the chapter's rule that the id lies between spreadsheets/d/ and /edit is safer written as a pattern match on the id's characters." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P02 .
chx:F5 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's account of quotas differs from the newest library and from Google's current page: the chapter says that EZSheets catches the quota exception and retries and that setting ezsheets.IGNORE_QUOTA to True makes the methods raise it, but in EZSheets 2026.4.28 the setting only switches off the library's own throttle of 90 requests in a window of 101 seconds, and the retry stays, with pauses of 10, 15, 20, 25, 30, 35, 40 and 45 seconds over nine attempts before the HttpError is raised, the same with the setting False and True; Google's page gives 300 read and 300 write requests a minute per project and 60 of each per user per project, asks for exponential backoff on HTTP 429, and states no daily limit when the per-minute quotas are respected, and the chapter's 250 new spreadsheets a day is not on it." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P02, chx:P03, chx:P04 .
chx:F6 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's set-up differs from current Google practice in three ways: the chapter's introduction speaks of two token JSON files while naming token-drive.pickle and token-sheets.pickle, which is what the library 2026.4.28 writes, where Google's Python quickstart keeps a token.json and needs Python 3.10.7 or later; unpickling can execute code, and the documentation says the pickle module is not secure, so a token file is as sensitive as a program, which the chapter's advice to treat it like a password covers only in part; and Google's scope table lists the Sheets scope as sensitive, the Drive scope as restricted and drive.file, which gives access only to the specific Drive files the user uses with the app, as the recommended non-sensitive scope, while the chapter asks for the full Drive and Sheets scopes." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P02, chx:P03, chx:P05, chx:P06, chx:P07, chx:P11 .
chx:F7 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The programs of Project 11 rest on Python facts the chapter does not spell out: cell values are text, so an amount needs int and an empty cell cannot be converted, which stops the audit loop on a blank row; the transaction program's check len(sys.argv) < 4 lets extra arguments through and they fail later with ValueError at the unpacking, and a name with a space is one argument only when quoted, as shlex.split shows; sheets is a tuple, so it is read and not changed in place; dict.setdefault creates an account at 0; a chained comparison ss1 == ss2 == ss3 means ss1 == ss2 and ss2 == ss3 with the compared-by-id equality the Spreadsheet class defines; and each write is a web request, so 100 single-cell writes are 100 requests where one updateColumn is one, while reads from a loaded sheet make none and setting rowCount makes three." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P02, chx:P12, chx:P13, chx:P14, chx:P17, chx:P18 .
chx:F8 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Conclusion"@en ; sen0414:findingText "For an advanced course, chapter 15 is best taught as a conversation with a remote system in which every name, address and write has a cost: a Spreadsheet and its Sheet objects are local copies refreshed on request, addresses count from 1 where lists count from 0, values are text, and the number of requests, not the number of lines, decides speed and quota; the chapter's outputs that do not depend on Google hold, but the printed returns of Sheet() and of a just-written number, the IGNORE_QUOTA account, the token files and the scopes differ in the newest library and in Google's pages, the library changes list arguments in place and cuts an id from a URL without a closing slash, and all the steps that need a browser login, an account or a form were read and not run." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P02, chx:P04, chx:P05, chx:P11 .
chx:Scorecard a sen0414:QualityScorecard ; rdfs:label "Quality scorecard"@en ; sen0414:topicCoverage "47 primary-source concepts inventoried from the textbook ontology." ; sen0414:citationStrength "18 sources, all opened and verified at 2026-10-01T12:00:00; 15 cited by findings." ; sen0414:methodologyCompliance "RDODI procedure v1.6.0 Stage 1." ; sen0414:reproducibility "Every source is a stable public URL; every claim names its sources." .
